A very persistent illusion, yeah.

01:20.140 --> 01:26.760
Yeah. What if the way we've been taught to look at information is fundamentally blinding us to the actual structure of reality?

01:27.260 --> 01:33.040
So we have a source today that offers a shortcut to a completely different way of seeing the universe.

01:33.160 --> 01:33.920
It really does.

01:33.920 --> 01:40.200
And I don't say that lightly. Today's deep dive is centered on a text that is, frankly, going to melt your brain a little bit.

01:40.420 --> 01:51.640
We are looking at a groundbreaking, highly theoretical 2026 book called Univalent Photonics, Coherence, Transport, and Admissibility Geometry by an author named Flixson.

01:51.640 --> 02:01.060
And we should probably establish right up front, while the title says photonics, and obviously we will be talking about the physics of light, this is not just an engineering manual about lasers or fiber optics.

02:01.080 --> 02:01.860
No, definitely not.

02:02.100 --> 02:07.540
Right. This text is on a mission to unpack a radical persistence-first view of physics.

02:07.880 --> 02:18.660
We're going to explore how treating light as a flowing structure of rules, rather than a sequence of numbers or a stream of localized signals, completely rewrites our understanding of computation.

02:18.660 --> 02:20.040
And reality, honestly.

02:20.200 --> 02:24.600
Exactly. Computation, reality, and what it fundamentally means for something to even exist.

02:24.940 --> 02:32.580
Okay, let's unpack this. Because the ambition of this book is just staggering. I mean, the author isn't just tweaking a formula here.

02:33.020 --> 02:41.320
They're trying to redefine identity itself, not just for a beam of light, but as a foundational concept for how the universe organizes itself.

02:41.560 --> 02:45.360
What's fascinating here is how Flixson methodically sets up this foundational shift.

02:45.360 --> 02:50.980
The text essentially demands that we stop asking what things are made of at any given frozen moment in time.

02:51.100 --> 02:52.020
Right, like taking a snapshot.

02:52.280 --> 02:57.140
Exactly. Stop taking snapshots. Instead, we have to start asking why things persist through time and space.

02:57.780 --> 03:08.220
But, you know, to truly understand this new persistence-first foundation, we first have to understand why the old foundation, the one you probably learned in high school physics, is fundamentally flawed.

03:08.220 --> 03:12.560
Yeah, the one that currently runs the entire tech industry, basically. Let's start exactly there.

03:12.720 --> 03:21.240
Because in high school, and even if you go on to study modern engineering, we're taught to think of light almost like a microscopic delivery truck.

03:21.360 --> 03:22.300
That's a good way to put it.

03:22.560 --> 03:28.300
We treat it as a carrier of signals. It's a sequence of ones and zeros flying down a fiber optic cable.

03:28.640 --> 03:32.620
Or it's a specific frequency wave waiting to be isolated and measured.

03:32.620 --> 03:35.960
The text calls this the sequential inheritance problem.

03:36.540 --> 03:39.580
And I was initially really confused by this term.

03:39.820 --> 03:41.120
Why inheritance?

03:41.840 --> 03:44.820
Do we inherit this view from classical mechanics?

03:45.500 --> 03:47.080
Actually, no, not classical mechanics.

03:47.300 --> 03:49.780
It's inherited from early electronic computation.

03:50.460 --> 03:54.120
And this is a crucial historical blind spot that Flixson points out.

03:54.240 --> 03:54.800
Okay, how so?

03:54.800 --> 04:00.900
Well, think about when modern photonics, the science of using light to transmit and process information, was really born.

04:00.900 --> 04:04.900
It was born alongside the electronic computer revolution in the mid-20th century.

04:05.200 --> 04:08.900
Early computers were built on sequential, symbolic logic.

04:09.620 --> 04:14.400
You had discrete electrical signals moving step-by-step through physical logic gates.

04:14.700 --> 04:16.640
It was all about local states.

04:16.940 --> 04:19.740
Like, is the switch on or is it off?

04:19.880 --> 04:22.160
Is the voltage high or is it low? That kind of thing.

04:22.400 --> 04:22.880
Precisely.

04:22.960 --> 04:24.600
All right, the classic von Neumann architecture.

04:24.780 --> 04:26.940
You fetch a piece of data, you process it, you store it.

04:27.200 --> 04:28.240
Step one, step two, step three.

04:28.240 --> 04:35.380
Exactly. And because that language of discrete, sequential computation became the absolute dominant paradigm of the era,

04:35.960 --> 04:41.020
we naturally applied that exact same language to light when we started building optical networks.

04:41.180 --> 04:41.660
Oh, I see.

